==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|abime}}, from {{der|en|fro|abisme}} from {{der|en|LL.|*abyssimus}}, a superlative of {{m|la|abyssus||bottomless pit}}, from {{der|en|grc|ἄβυσσος}}. Cognate to {{cog|fr|abîme}}. See also {{m|en|abyss}}.<ref name=OCD>{{R:OCD2|page=6}}</ref><ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=11}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈbɪzəm/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-abysm.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪzəm|s=3}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|archaic|poetic}} [[hell|Hell]]; the infernal pit; the great deep; the primal [[chaos]]. {{defdate|from between 1150 and 1350}}<ref name=SOED/>
#* {{quote-journal |en |year=2025 |last=Ross |first=Peter |title=Discover the Astounding Secrets of Scotland’s Stone Age Settlements. In the Orkney Islands, archaeologists close the chapter on a legendary excavation, capping two decades of remarkable Neolithic discoveries |journal=Smithsonian |volume=55 |issue=7: January/February |url=https://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/discover-astounding-secrets-scotlands-stone-age-settlements-180985627/ |passage=Orkney is an archipelago of 70 or so islands lying off the northeast tip of Scotland. “Everywhere in Orkney there is the sense of age, the dark backward and '''abysm''',” the late Orcadian poet George Mackay Brown wrote. “The islands have been inhabited for a very long time, from before the day of the plough.”}}
# {{lb|en|now|_|chiefly|literary}} An [[abyss]]; a gulf, a [[chasm]], a very deep hole. {{defdate|from late 15th century}}<ref name=SOED/>
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1623|author=Shakespeare|title=Antony and Cleopatra|section=III, xiii
|passage=The '''abysm''' of hell.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1946|author=w:Mervyn Peake|title=w:Titus Groan|location=London|publisher=Eyre & Spottiswoode|chapter=The Grotto
|passage=Dr. Prunesquallor had circled around Steerpike with his head drawn back so that his cervical vertebrae rested against the near wall of his high collar, and a plumbless '''abysm''' yawned between his Adam’s apple and his pearl stud.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Glyn Maxwell|title=‘Ideas of Order,’ by Neil L. Rudenstine &#91;book review; print version: Eternal lines: A guide to Shakespeare's sonnets, ''International New York Times'', 2 February 2015, p.&nbsp;7&#93;|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2015/02/01/books/review/ideas-of-order-by-neil-l-rudenstine.html|work=w:The New York Times|date=30 January 2015|passage=[T]he Shakespearean sonnet doesn't lend itself to a sequential narrative, because the rhymed couplet, without its paired feet trembling at that '''abysm''' of time, has to settle instead for the sound of sighing resolution, at regular intervals, over and over, before taking a deep breath and returning usually, for better or worse, to the same subject.}}

